        ['q' => 'What does a public adjuster do?', 'a' => '<p>A public adjuster is licensed to represent the policyholder, not the insurer, on a property insurance claim. The work usually includes inspecting the damage, reading the policy, preparing a detailed estimate and supporting documents, presenting the claim, and negotiating with the insurance company. In Texas, public adjusters are licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance under Insurance Code Chapter 4102. See <a href="/what-is-a-public-adjuster-and-how-to-hire-one/">what is a public adjuster and how to hire one</a>.</p>'],
        ['q' => 'Do I need a public adjuster for my claim?', 'a' => '<p>Not always. Small, simple claims are often settled fairly without help. A San Marcos public adjuster is usually most useful when the loss is large or complicated, the claim was denied or underpaid, the insurer\'s estimate leaves out damage, or you do not have time to manage the process. Our guide <a href="/do-i-need-a-public-adjuster/">do I need a public adjuster?</a> walks through the decision.</p>'],
        ['q' => 'How much does a public adjuster cost in Texas?', 'a' => '<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If the insurer pays, or commits in writing to pay, policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, a percentage fee is not allowed. Our <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> costs nothing. For a closer look at value, read <a href="/are-public-adjusters-worth-it/">are public adjusters worth it?</a></p>'],
